{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q04",
  "speechText": "Recently, I worked on an AI agent workflow system called StrangeLoop. It has a Studio for configuration and an Engine for execution. The Engine runs the workflow step by step. It can call the LLM, use different tools, and make decisions along the way. The app still controls its own data and backend actions. So it is easier to control and debug. For approval, if a tool needs it, the Engine stops and waits for the user before calling the backend. For data, each run only gets the data it needs. If the scope is wrong, the system returns nothing. For tracking, I keep clear logs with the important IDs, status, errors, and performance metrics, such as latency, processing time, and memory usage. This helps me monitor the system, find what went wrong, and debug problems more easily. Then, we use those insights to improve the workflow, prompts, or tools over time. So overall, a good agent system needs clear boundaries. The platform controls the rules and orchestration. The application controls its own data and business actions.",
